Z||000009||Jaygee79||09-14-2001||01:16 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by kempo:<BR><B>educate me...<BR>what is the meaning of having a flag half-mast?  silly question, but i'm really confused here...  thanks.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>its something you do when the country is in mourning.  i found a website that lists when it should be flown at half mast and for how long, if anyone would like me to post it, let me know.<P>i was going to go give blood, but that's out of the question now.  i want to put a flag on my antenna but i don't know where to get one and i'm sure wal-mart will be out of them by now.<p>[This message has been edited by Jaygee79 (edited 09-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||09-14-2001||01:21 AM||||When I went to go play basketball with my friends the other night, I had a flag on my antenna. All they did was laugh, and say "nice flag" sarcastically. I was pretty pissed, but was also surprised and sad from the response.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif">         <BR>^^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^^<BR><LI><FONT SIZE="1">84 SE (FOR SALE)*sale pending*<BR><LI>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR><LI>86 SC (FOR SALE)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||09-14-2001||03:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jaygee79:<BR><B>   its something you do when the country is in mourning.  i found a website that lists when it should be flown at half mast and for how long, if anyone would like me to post it, let me know.<P>i was going to go give blood, but that's out of the question now.  i want to put a flag on my antenna but i don't know where to get one and i'm sure wal-mart will be out of them by now.<P>[This message has been edited by Jaygee79 (edited 09-14-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Please post the link.  I'd like to read exactly when it's appropriate, and for how long.  I know it's a metaphor for the country bowing it's head in respect and honor and a way to show the country's mouring, but don't know the specifics.<P><P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><I><FONT COLOR="blue">The path of the righteous man is beset on all sides by the inequities of the selfish and the tyrranys of evil men. Blessed is he who in the name of charity and good will shepards the weak through the valley of darkness for he is truly his brothers keeper, and the finder of lost children. And I shall strike down upon thee with great vengence and furious anger, those who attempt to poison and destroy my brothers. And you will know my name is the lord when I lay my vengence upon thee. </I></FONT></FONT><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/9-11-01a.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Jaygee79||09-14-2001||03:39 PM||||<B>When should the flag be flown at half-staff?</B><P><BR>A relatively easy way to remember when to fly the United States flag at half-staff is to consider when the whole nation is in mourning. These periods of mourning are proclaimed either by the president of the United States, for national remembrance, or the governor of a state or territory, for local remembrance, in the event of a death of a member or former member of the federal, state or territorial government or judiciary. The heads of departments and agencies of the federal government may also order that the flag be flown at half-staff on buildings, grounds and naval vessels under their jurisdiction. <P>On Memorial day the flag should be flown at half-staff from sunrise until noon only, then raised briskly to the top of the staff until sunset, in honor of the nation's battle heroes. <P>In the early days of our country, no regulations existed for flying the flag at half-staff and, as a result, there were many conflicting policies. But on March 1, 1954, President Dwight Eisenhower issued a proclamation on the proper times. <P>The flag should fly at half-staff for 30 days at all federal buildings, grounds, and naval vessels throughout the United States and its territories and possessions after the death of the president or a former president. It is to fly 10 days at half-staff after the death of the vice president, the chief justice or a retired chief justice of the United States Supreme Court, or the speaker of the House of Representatives. For an associate justice of the Supreme Court, a member of the Cabinet, a former vice president, the president pro tempore of the Senate, the majority leader of the Senate, the minority leader of the Senate, the majority leader of the House of Representatives, or the minority leader of the House of Representatives the flag is to be displayed at half-staff from the day of death until interment. <P>The flag is to be flown at half-staff at all federal buildings, grounds and naval vessels in the Washington, D.C., area on the day and day after the death of a United States senator, representative, territorial delegate, or the resident commissioner from the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico. It shall also be flown at half-staff on all federal facilities in the state, congressional district, territory, or commonwealth of these officials. <P>Upon the death of the governor of a state, territory or possession, the flag shall be flown at half-staff on all federal facilities in that governor's state, territory or possession from the day of death until interment. <P>The president may order the flag to be flown at half-staff to mark the death of other officials, former officials, or foreign dignitaries. In addition to these occasions, the president may order half-staff display of the flag after other tragic events. <P>The flag should be briskly run up to the top of the staff before being lowered slowly to the half-staff position.<P>Here is the link where I got this:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.va.gov/pubaff/celebAm/halfstaf.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.va.gov/pubaff/celebAm/halfstaf.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||fierobaby||09-14-2001||03:42 PM||||well opps!<p>[This message has been edited by fierobaby (edited 09-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||stsmithgt||09-14-2001||05:20 PM||||To keep it half way up the antenna, I would use small plastic zip ties to hold it in place.  I'm trying a 4" long tie to see how it'll work when I get my break this afternoon.  I'm sure it'll work.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I'll use two to hold the flag spread open and two others to hold the flag onto the antenna.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfupflag.gif">  Oops, messy picture. Oh well. <BR> Here's an image where I'll be putting my zip ties.  The red ones, will spread and hold the position on the antenna.  The blue ones will hold the flag to the antenna.  I think they have to be tight enough to not travel over the red ones or the flag will end up on the ground. <P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 09-14-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000015||stsmithgt||09-14-2001||06:47 PM||||Tip.....Those little flags aren't assembled very well.  I applied super glue on the seams to keep it from shreading at those freeway speeds.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/antennaflag.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfmaston_antenna.jpg">  Realy, it's at half mast though it looks way up there.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/nrasticker.jpg">   OHHHHHHHHHHhhhh yeahhhhhhhhhhhh.<P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 09-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||09-14-2001||07:13 PM||||I tried to find an American flag, but can't find any anywhere.  My wife suggested something in the fabric section at Wally World.  I found some material that, when folded in half, shows a different flag on each side.  Neither is the official flag, but both are close.  One side is "Life, Liberty & the Pursuit of Happiness" and the other is the beginning of the Pledge of Allegiance.  Not exactly what I was looking for but close enough.  Only problem is that it's too much to hang on my antenna while I'm driving.  I'm afraid it will break the antenna.  I might try hanging it in the rear window.  I'll snap some pics once I get new batteries for my POS digicam. ||||reg||1
